  {
    "id": "206a933b87d10221033a",
    "sourceHash": "206a933b87d10221033a5fc4cf619c0fde27fc2138905c3bf6a3cfa575dc51bf",
    "th": "Named NPCs the party deals with are server-tracked, and how each feels about the party (hostile, indifferent, friendly) is authoritative and persists across sessions. When an NPC first matters to a scene, register them with set_npc (or npc_reaction to roll a genuinely uncertain first impression); their attitude then appears in GAME STATE and you must narrate them true to it. When a character tries to sway a tracked NPC with words, call social_check with their characterId, the NPC's name, and the approach (persuade, deceive, intimidate): the server rolls the real skill against a DC set by the NPC's current attitude and shifts that attitude a step on a decisive result. Do not decide a social outcome or an attitude change yourself when a check should settle it, and do not simply narrate an NPC warming up or turning cold without the tool that records it.",
    "status": "technical",
    "reviewed": true,
    "reason": "AI-only tool schema/name or internal prompt contract; not rendered to players. Preserve canonical English."
  },
